{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_00436256a65124c50169bb35ce4f6c819186ffc2fbe938ac5c", "created": 1773876686, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Lymph Nodes in Cancer Spread, Staging, Biopsy, and Nodal Surgery\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community is centered on LYMPH NODES as both immune “filter” structures and as clinically critical sites for cancer spread, staging, recurrence, and treatment decision-making. The network connects lymph nodes to (1) core biological functions (filtering out foreign particles and harmful cells), (2) clinical assessment locations (neck, underarms/armpits), (3) diagnostic confirmation methods (needle biopsy and excisional biopsy), (4) staging and operative management (tumor-and-node removal, lymph node dissection, lymph node status, TNM N category, extranodal extension), and (5) specific cancer contexts where nodal involvement defines “regional” disease or recurrence patterns (e.g., squamous cell skin cancer, prostate cancer) as well as primary nodal malignancies (B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma). Overall, the entities describe a medical decision-support cluster where lymph-node evaluation is repeatedly positioned as pivotal to determining extent of disease and selecting treatment pathways. [Data: Entities (33, 3915, 1656, 3512, 6197); Relationships (8125, 546, 4714, 7143, 14194,+more)]\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Lymph nodes are positioned as the central hub linking immune filtering with cancer spread and evaluation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"LYMPH NODES are described as small immune/lymphatic structures that filter lymph, help fight infection, and remove foreign or harmful material (including cancer cells), establishing a functional basis for why they are routinely assessed in clinical care. This “filter” framing connects directly to downstream diagnostic and staging decisions when suspicious or enlarged nodes raise concern for malignant involvement. [Data: Entities (33, 3915, 1232, 1233, 1661); Relationships (8125, 1983, 1984, 2749, 2806)]\\n\\nThe same central role extends into oncology: lymph nodes are repeatedly referenced as sites where cancer can spread and as inputs to staging systems and treatment selection, making them a key connector between detection (exam/imaging/biopsy) and management (surgery, radiation, systemic therapy). [Data: Entities (33, 441, 1656, 3512, 1700); Relationships (547, 7143, 2773, 4714, 1246)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Physical examination prioritizes high-yield nodal regions such as the neck and underarms/armpits\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The NECK is explicitly highlighted as a common and important region where lymph nodes are assessed by palpation for swelling/enlargement, reflecting a practical clinical workflow where accessible nodal basins are checked first during evaluation of possible infection or malignancy. [Data: Relationships (335)]\\n\\nThe UNDERARMS and ARMPITS are also emphasized as areas with high concentrations of lymph nodes and as exam targets for enlargement, reinforcing that the community’s diagnostic pathway begins with anatomical screening at typical nodal sites. [Data: Entities (3428, 1226); Relationships (6874, 1982)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Biopsy pathways range from minimally invasive needle sampling to surgical excisional biopsy when needed\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Needle biopsy is directly linked to lymph node testing when nodal spread is suspected, indicating a common first-line approach to obtain tissue confirmation while minimizing invasiveness. [Data: Entities (434); Relationships (546)]\\n\\nExcisional biopsy is described as a surgical technique involving cutting into the skin to remove all or part of a lymph node; it is positioned as an option when less invasive methods are not feasible or when results conflict with clinical expectations. This creates an escalation pathway within the community from sampling (needle) to more definitive surgical tissue acquisition (excisional). [Data: Entities (3781, 6064); Relationships (13828)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Nodal involvement is tightly integrated with staging concepts, including lymph node status and the TNM ‘N’ category\",\n      \"explanation\": \"LYMPH NODE STATUS is characterized as a summary of whether cancer has spread to lymph nodes (including number and location of involved nodes) and is framed as a key element used in cancer staging and treatment planning, especially when established through post-surgical pathologic assessment. [Data: Entities (1656); Relationships (9831)]\\n\\nThe TNM framework explicitly includes an N component describing lymph node spread, reinforcing that nodal assessment is not ancillary but structurally embedded in formal staging systems. In the provided context, N CATEGORY (N) is described for anal cancer as capturing whether nodes near the anus contain cancer. [Data: Entities (3512); Relationships (7143)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Surgical management of nodes spans staging-oriented removal and more extensive lymph node dissection\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Tumor and lymph node removal is described as including removal of nearby lymph nodes as part of pathologic staging and post-surgical assessment, indicating that nodal surgery can be diagnostic as well as therapeutic. [Data: Entities (2570); Relationships (4714)]\\n\\nLYMPH NODE DISSECTION is described as a more extensive operation (typically under general anesthesia) in which multiple lymph nodes are removed and examined, and it is referenced both in general cancer management and in specific recurrence scenarios. This differentiates targeted biopsy from broader nodal clearance and evaluation. [Data: Entities (578); Relationships (14252)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Recurrence patterns include ‘cancer returning to lymph nodes,’ with management linked to feasibility of additional radiation and surgical options\",\n      \"explanation\": \"CANCER RETURNING TO LYMPH NODES is defined as recurrence found in lymph nodes during surveillance, establishing a specific follow-up failure mode that can trigger additional therapy. [Data: Entities (6197); Relationships (14194)]\\n\\nIn this recurrence setting, lymph node dissection is explicitly connected as a potential need when further radiation is not used or not possible, and it may function as a standalone procedure depending on the broader surgical plan. This ties surveillance findings to concrete operative decision points. [Data: Entities (578); Relationships (14252)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"For squamous cell skin cancer, lymph node spread delineates ‘local’ versus ‘regional’ disease and drives node-focused testing\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community defines LOCAL SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER as disease that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes, while REGIONAL SQUAMOUS CELL SKIN CANCER is defined as cancer found in lymph nodes. This creates a clear nodal threshold separating clinical categories. [Data: Entities (442, 443); Relationships (548, 549)]\\n\\nLYMPH NODE TESTING is linked to suspicious nodes to determine extent of spread and inform staging and treatment selection, reinforcing that once nodal involvement is suspected, structured evaluation of nodes becomes a central management activity. [Data: Entities (863, 441); Relationships (1246, 547)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Extranodal extension is treated as an important descriptor of disease behavior beyond the lymph node boundary\",\n      \"explanation\": \"EXTRANODAL EXTENSION is explicitly defined as cancer spreading outside of the lymph node, adding a layer of severity/extent beyond simply whether a node is positive. [Data: Entities (1700); Relationships (2773)]\\n\\nIn practical terms, this concept complements nodal positivity by describing how cancer behaves at and beyond nodal capsules, supporting more granular characterization of nodal disease rather than a binary involved/not-involved framing. [Data: Entities (1700, 441); Relationships (2773, 547)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Some malignancies are defined by predominant lymph node involvement, including B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma presentations\",\n      \"explanation\": \"B-CELL LYMPHOBLASTIC LYMPHOMA (B-LL/B-LBL) (including the alternate labeled entry) is characterized as a malignancy in which B-cell lymphoblast proliferation occurs predominantly in lymph nodes rather than primarily in bone marrow, explicitly anchoring disease identity to nodal localization. [Data: Entities (2611, 2914); Relationships (4784, 5419)]\\n\\nB-CELL LYMPHOBLASTS are described as sometimes growing mostly in lymph nodes instead of bone marrow, mechanistically supporting why a lymphoblastic process may be designated as lymphoma when nodal disease predominates. [Data: Entities (3042); Relationships (5978)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Procedure- and anatomy-specific node concepts extend to thyroid-region neck dissection and stomach curvatures with abundant nodes\",\n      \"explanation\": \"NECK DISSECTION is directly linked to LYMPH NODES and described as removing lymph nodes near the thyroid due to known or suspected cancer involvement, illustrating a specialized nodal surgery tightly tied to a particular anatomic region and cancer-management workflow. [Data: Entities (5022); Relationships (11066)]\\n\\nSeparately, many lymph nodes are described as being found along the lesser and greater curvatures of the stomach, indicating that nodal anatomy is also mapped to organ-specific regions relevant for gastrointestinal cancer evaluation and surgery. [Data: Entities (5439, 5440); Relationships (12077, 12078)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 7.2,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"Impact severity is high because lymph-node involvement is repeatedly presented as a pivotal de